\title{gsbundle: Gene Set Retrieval and Gene ID Conversion Utilities}
\description{
gsbundle provides a unified interface for downloading and caching gene sets
from public databases, converting gene identifiers across formats and
organisms, and running gene set enrichment analyses.
}
\section{Gene set retrieval}{

Use \code{\link[=gsGetter]{gsGetter()}} to fetch gene–pathway mappings from KEGG, Reactome,
Gene Ontology, MSigDB, or CORUM. Results are cached locally in
\code{tools::R_user_dir("gsbundle", "cache")} and reused on subsequent calls.
Call \code{\link[=retrieve_gs]{retrieve_gs()}} to force a re-download, and \code{\link[=gs_versions]{gs_versions()}} to
inspect the version of each cached database.
}

\section{Gene ID conversion}{

\code{\link[=TransGeneID]{TransGeneID()}} converts a character vector of gene identifiers between
Entrez, Symbol, Ensembl, and UniProt formats, within a single organism or
across organisms (e.g. mouse → human). Cross-organism mapping uses
ortholog tables from MGI, NCBI HomoloGene, and Ensembl. Annotation data
is fetched via \code{\link[=getGeneAnn]{getGeneAnn()}} (within-organism) and \code{\link[=getOrtAnn]{getOrtAnn()}}
(cross-organism).
}

\section{Enrichment analysis}{

\code{\link[=runEnrich]{runEnrich()}} wraps \pkg{clusterProfiler} to run hypergeometric
(\code{"HGT"}), over-representation (\code{"ORT"}), or gene set enrichment
(\code{"GSEA"}) tests against any gene set table returned by \code{\link[=gsGetter]{gsGetter()}}.
Redundant pathways can be grouped or filtered using DBSCAN clustering
(\code{\link[=cluster.dbscan]{cluster.dbscan()}}) or a pairwise Jaccard cutoff (\code{\link[=EnrichedFilter]{EnrichedFilter()}}).
}

\section{Supported organisms}{

Human (\code{hsa}), mouse (\code{mmu}), rat (\code{rno}), bovine (\code{bta}),
canine (\code{cfa}), chimpanzee (\code{ptr}), pig (\code{ssc}).
}

